1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,880
You leave a banana on the kitchen counter
and forget about it for a few days.

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:08,620
It goes brown. Then black.
It starts to smell.

3
00:00:08,974 --> 00:00:12,294
And then, one morning,
there are tiny flies circling it.

4
00:00:12,794 --> 00:00:15,954
A few days ago,
there wasn't a single fly in the kitchen.

5
00:00:16,034 --> 00:00:18,394
So where did they come from?

6
00:00:18,687 --> 00:00:21,467
Did you just witness life
appearing from nothing?

7
00:00:22,311 --> 00:00:23,071
They're fruit flies.

8
00:00:23,301 --> 00:00:27,971
And they're drawn to fermenting fruit,
and to the yeast growing on it.

9
00:00:28,431 --> 00:00:30,731
The smell can pull them in from outside.

10
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:33,546
Some slip in through a gap in a window.

11
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:37,226
Some ride in as eggs
on other fruit you brought home.

12
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:39,709
And once a few are in, they breed.

13
00:00:40,509 --> 00:00:44,589
In warm conditions, it takes about
ten days from egg to flying adult.

14
00:00:44,929 --> 00:00:47,749
And one female can lay
around five hundred eggs.

15
00:00:48,233 --> 00:00:50,393
So nothing appeared out of nowhere.

16
00:00:50,713 --> 00:00:54,593
Every one of those flies
hatched from an egg laid by another fly.

17
00:00:55,107 --> 00:00:56,647
That sounds obvious.

18
00:00:57,007 --> 00:01:00,727
But it took people more than two
thousand years to work it out.

19
00:01:01,127 --> 00:01:04,527
And the answer left behind
a problem we still haven't solved.

20
00:01:05,119 --> 00:01:07,679
Because if life only ever
comes from life...

21
00:01:07,759 --> 00:01:09,759
where did the first life come from?

22
00:01:12,289 --> 00:01:15,969
For most of history, the answer
to the kitchen mystery was much simpler.

23
00:01:16,729 --> 00:01:18,849
Life could just start on its own.

24
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:23,927
More than two thousand years ago,
Aristotle wrote that eels grew out of mud,

25
00:01:24,067 --> 00:01:27,167
and that some insects formed
from dew falling on leaves.

26
00:01:27,899 --> 00:01:32,679
The idea is called spontaneous generation,
and it lasted for centuries.

27
00:01:33,656 --> 00:01:37,636
In the 1600s, the Flemish chemist
Jan Baptist van Helmont

28
00:01:37,866 --> 00:01:40,076
even wrote down a recipe for mice.

29
00:01:40,392 --> 00:01:42,852
Put some dirty underwear
in a jar of wheat.

30
00:01:42,932 --> 00:01:46,892
Wait twenty-one days.
And mice will appear.

31
00:01:47,396 --> 00:01:49,376
And to be fair, it looks convincing.

32
00:01:50,096 --> 00:01:52,936
Leave grain in a barn,
and mice eventually appear.

33
00:01:53,096 --> 00:01:55,376
But the barn already had mice.

34
00:01:57,766 --> 00:02:01,986
In 1668, an Italian physician
called Francesco Redi

35
00:02:02,066 --> 00:02:04,846
decided to stop arguing
and start watching.

36
00:02:05,257 --> 00:02:06,737
He put pieces of meat into jars.

37
00:02:06,887 --> 00:02:08,337
Some he left open.

38
00:02:08,417 --> 00:02:10,057
Some he sealed shut.

39
00:02:10,137 --> 00:02:12,777
The open jars filled with maggots.

40
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:14,797
The sealed ones didn't.

41
00:02:15,077 --> 00:02:16,597
But there was an obvious objection.

42
00:02:16,827 --> 00:02:20,377
Sealing the jar also cut off the air.

43
00:02:20,897 --> 00:02:23,277
Maybe life needed fresh air to start.

44
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:26,727
So Redi covered some jars
with fine gauze instead.

45
00:02:26,847 --> 00:02:30,087
Air could get in. Flies couldn't.

46
00:02:30,414 --> 00:02:32,094
No maggots in the meat.

47
00:02:32,234 --> 00:02:36,914
But maggots on top of the gauze, dropped
there by flies that couldn't get in.

48
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:40,325
The meat wasn't making life.
The flies were.

49
00:02:42,555 --> 00:02:45,915
That should have settled it.
But a few years later,

50
00:02:45,995 --> 00:02:49,235
microscopes revealed a whole world
of tiny living things.

51
00:02:49,691 --> 00:02:53,751
And they seemed to turn up in any broth
left out, with no eggs anyone could see.

52
00:02:54,331 --> 00:02:57,411
So maybe spontaneous generation
still worked...

53
00:02:57,491 --> 00:02:58,691
just smaller.

54
00:03:00,851 --> 00:03:04,691
Almost two hundred years after Redi,
in 1859,

55
00:03:04,771 --> 00:03:07,311
Louis Pasteur took on the question
in Paris.

56
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:10,790
He filled glass flasks with broth,
heated their necks

57
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:14,230
and bent them into a long curve,
like the neck of a swan.

58
00:03:14,453 --> 00:03:17,693
Then he boiled the broth
to kill anything already in it.

59
00:03:18,273 --> 00:03:20,733
Air could still get in
through the open end.

60
00:03:21,077 --> 00:03:23,217
But the dust and microbes
drifting in with it

61
00:03:23,447 --> 00:03:26,757
settled in the bend of the neck,
and never reached the broth.

62
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:30,785
The broth stayed clear.
For months. For years.

63
00:03:31,489 --> 00:03:35,269
Then he broke the neck off a flask,
so the dust could fall straight in.

64
00:03:35,869 --> 00:03:37,789
Within a day or two, it was cloudy.

65
00:03:38,985 --> 00:03:42,825
In 1864, Pasteur told an audience
at the Sorbonne

66
00:03:43,055 --> 00:03:46,445
that spontaneous generation
would never recover from this blow.

67
00:03:47,481 --> 00:03:49,081
Life comes from life.

68
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:52,781
And that is exactly
what's happening on your kitchen counter.

69
00:03:55,391 --> 00:03:57,851
But look at what that did
to the bigger question.

70
00:03:58,823 --> 00:04:01,283
Around the same time,
Charles Darwin argued

71
00:04:01,513 --> 00:04:04,703
that every living thing
descends from earlier living things.

72
00:04:05,225 --> 00:04:08,185
Put Pasteur and Darwin together,
and you get a chain.

73
00:04:08,645 --> 00:04:11,965
Life from life, back and back,
for billions of years...

74
00:04:12,065 --> 00:04:13,505
and it can't go on forever.

75
00:04:15,185 --> 00:04:18,305
Earth formed about
four and a half billion years ago.

76
00:04:19,065 --> 00:04:22,105
At first it was far too hot
for anything to survive.

77
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:24,899
The oldest widely accepted traces of life

78
00:04:25,129 --> 00:04:27,999
are about
three and a half billion years old.

79
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:33,019
Layered rocks in Western Australia,
built up by mats of microbes.

80
00:04:33,699 --> 00:04:36,779
So at some point,
on a planet with no life at all,

81
00:04:36,859 --> 00:04:40,059
something that wasn't alive
became something that was.

82
00:04:40,907 --> 00:04:43,527
Scientists call that step abiogenesis.

83
00:04:44,287 --> 00:04:47,327
And it seems to clash
with everything Pasteur proved.

84
00:04:47,823 --> 00:04:51,123
But Pasteur never showed
that life can't come from chemistry.

85
00:04:51,983 --> 00:04:55,783
He showed it doesn't happen
in a swan-neck flask, even after years.

86
00:04:57,613 --> 00:05:02,293
The early Earth had hundreds of millions
of years, and whole oceans to work with.

87
00:05:02,533 --> 00:05:04,693
And it had one more advantage.

88
00:05:05,298 --> 00:05:08,358
Darwin spotted it in a letter in 1871.

89
00:05:09,158 --> 00:05:13,738
Today, he wrote, any chemistry on its way
to life would be devoured instantly.

90
00:05:14,812 --> 00:05:18,812
Your banana never stood a chance.
Yeast got to it first.

91
00:05:19,332 --> 00:05:21,292
Then the flies came for the yeast.

92
00:05:21,795 --> 00:05:24,895
Before the first life,
there was nothing to get there first.

93
00:05:27,705 --> 00:05:31,725
In that same letter, Darwin began
with a warning: oh, what a big if.

94
00:05:32,385 --> 00:05:34,165
Then he imagined a warm little pond,

95
00:05:34,395 --> 00:05:37,645
with ammonia and salts,
light, heat and electricity.

96
00:05:39,133 --> 00:05:42,493
In 1952, a student called Stanley Miller,

97
00:05:42,573 --> 00:05:45,413
working with Harold Urey,
tried to fill in that if.

98
00:05:45,713 --> 00:05:49,333
He sealed water, methane,
ammonia and hydrogen in glass,

99
00:05:49,413 --> 00:05:53,313
heated it, and fired sparks through it
to stand in for lightning.

100
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:56,440
After a week,
the water had turned a murky red.

101
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:00,520
And in it were amino acids,
the building blocks of proteins.

102
00:06:01,566 --> 00:06:04,946
In 2008, researchers re-tested vials

103
00:06:05,176 --> 00:06:06,866
from a steamier version of his experiment,

104
00:06:07,096 --> 00:06:10,106
and found more than twenty amino acids.

105
00:06:10,814 --> 00:06:14,634
Miller's gases were probably not
quite what the early atmosphere held.

106
00:06:14,714 --> 00:06:17,794
But other mixtures
make building blocks too.

107
00:06:18,374 --> 00:06:19,574
And so does space.

108
00:06:20,207 --> 00:06:25,047
In 1969, a meteorite fell
near Murchison, in Australia.

109
00:06:25,587 --> 00:06:29,127
Inside it were more than seventy
kinds of amino acids.

110
00:06:29,684 --> 00:06:32,264
So the ingredients aren't the hard part.

111
00:06:32,804 --> 00:06:35,884
Chemistry makes them on its own,
on Earth and off it.

112
00:06:36,838 --> 00:06:38,858
The hard part is the next step.

113
00:06:39,598 --> 00:06:44,258
A heap of building blocks isn't alive, the
same way a bag of letters isn't a book.

114
00:06:44,915 --> 00:06:46,995
Life has to carry instructions.

115
00:06:47,715 --> 00:06:49,295
And it has to copy them.

116
00:06:50,055 --> 00:06:52,135
In your cells, that's a team effort.

117
00:06:52,915 --> 00:06:54,675
DNA stores the instructions.

118
00:06:55,335 --> 00:06:58,195
Proteins do the work,
including copying the DNA.

119
00:06:58,723 --> 00:07:01,343
But proteins are built
from DNA's instructions.

120
00:07:02,283 --> 00:07:06,703
And DNA is copied by proteins.
So which came first?

121
00:07:08,183 --> 00:07:12,463
In 1982, Thomas Cech found a molecule
that broke the loop.

122
00:07:13,243 --> 00:07:16,203
A piece of RNA, DNA's chemical cousin,

123
00:07:16,283 --> 00:07:19,543
that could cut and join itself
with no protein involved.

124
00:07:20,535 --> 00:07:23,255
RNA can carry instructions, like DNA.

125
00:07:23,335 --> 00:07:25,675
And some RNA can do work, like a protein.

126
00:07:25,905 --> 00:07:28,815
One molecule, both jobs.

127
00:07:29,593 --> 00:07:32,613
That's the idea behind
what's called the RNA world.

128
00:07:33,193 --> 00:07:38,173
Early life may have run on RNA,
before DNA and proteins took over.

129
00:07:38,493 --> 00:07:41,193
And there's a clue
inside every one of your cells.

130
00:07:41,753 --> 00:07:44,673
Every protein in your body
was put together by a machine

131
00:07:44,903 --> 00:07:46,873
whose working heart is RNA.

132
00:07:48,079 --> 00:07:53,379
In 2026, researchers reported
a tiny RNA called QT45.

133
00:07:54,099 --> 00:07:57,519
From ready-made pieces,
it can build a copy of itself.

134
00:07:58,299 --> 00:08:03,279
But no RNA has yet been shown
to keep copying itself, round after round.

135
00:08:03,881 --> 00:08:05,841
And nobody knows where it happened.

136
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:09,361
A warm pond,
drying out and filling up again?

137
00:08:09,561 --> 00:08:12,281
Or hot vents on the dark ocean floor?

138
00:08:12,634 --> 00:08:15,934
Each idea has serious scientists
behind it.

139
00:08:16,414 --> 00:08:18,374
None of them has the whole answer.

140
00:08:20,944 --> 00:08:24,204
But maybe the question itself
is set up the wrong way.

141
00:08:25,216 --> 00:08:27,256
Where did the first life come from?

142
00:08:27,656 --> 00:08:29,496
That assumes there was a first.

143
00:08:30,196 --> 00:08:33,496
One moment, one thing,
that wasn't alive, and then was.

144
00:08:33,628 --> 00:08:35,368
So what counts as alive?

145
00:08:36,188 --> 00:08:39,008
One working definition,
developed by NASA scientists,

146
00:08:39,238 --> 00:08:42,688
describes life as
a self-sustaining chemical system

147
00:08:42,918 --> 00:08:45,048
capable of Darwinian evolution.

148
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:49,290
Picture a molecule
that makes rough copies of itself.

149
00:08:50,010 --> 00:08:52,210
Some copies come out a little different.

150
00:08:52,604 --> 00:08:54,904
Some of them copy faster than others.

151
00:08:55,664 --> 00:09:00,164
And if those differences are inherited,
the faster copies become more common.

152
00:09:00,787 --> 00:09:04,647
Is that alive? It's not a cell.
It doesn't eat or breathe.

153
00:09:04,947 --> 00:09:06,307
But it's already evolving.

154
00:09:07,337 --> 00:09:09,157
Now add a membrane around it.

155
00:09:09,237 --> 00:09:11,217
Then a way to grab energy.

156
00:09:11,637 --> 00:09:13,357
Then a better copier.

157
00:09:13,437 --> 00:09:15,497
At which step did it come alive?

158
00:09:16,542 --> 00:09:19,582
It's a bit like asking
who spoke the first word of French.

159
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:21,482
Nobody did.

160
00:09:21,882 --> 00:09:26,022
Spoken Latin just slowly drifted,
until one day it was French.

161
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:29,513
Life may be the same. Not a spark.

162
00:09:29,855 --> 00:09:31,055
A slope.

163
00:09:31,170 --> 00:09:34,190
Chemistry that got a little more
like life, again and again,

164
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:35,990
until there was no going back.

165
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:40,940
And here's the strangest part.

166
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:46,620
The fly, the banana, the yeast and you
all use essentially the same genetic code.

167
00:09:46,987 --> 00:09:49,127
That points to a common ancestor.

168
00:09:49,887 --> 00:09:53,327
But a common ancestor
isn't necessarily the first life.

169
00:09:53,797 --> 00:09:56,557
It doesn't tell us
whether life began only once,

170
00:09:56,697 --> 00:09:59,697
or whether other beginnings
simply left no survivors.

171
00:10:00,257 --> 00:10:04,537
The best way to find out may be to find
life that started somewhere else.

172
00:10:06,997 --> 00:10:10,697
So next time fruit flies turn up
around a forgotten banana,

173
00:10:10,817 --> 00:10:13,077
they're not life appearing from nothing.

174
00:10:13,385 --> 00:10:16,065
They're the newest link
in a chain that hasn't broken once

175
00:10:16,295 --> 00:10:18,865
in about three and a half billion years.

176
00:10:19,197 --> 00:10:22,117
And at the far end of that chain,
there's no first fly.

177
00:10:22,197 --> 00:10:24,437
Maybe no first anything.

178
00:10:24,877 --> 00:10:28,237
Just chemistry,
slowly getting better at copying itself.

179
00:10:31,387 --> 00:10:33,247
So here's a question for you.

180
00:10:33,967 --> 00:10:37,287
If a lab one day made something
that copies itself and evolves,

181
00:10:37,467 --> 00:10:41,127
starting from nothing but simple
chemicals, would you call it alive?

182
00:10:41,867 --> 00:10:43,297
Let me know in the comments.
